omponent Mapping\n38\t\n39\tAll epic `component` values are verbatim canonical names from\n40\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`. RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 (documentation uplift)\n41\tuses the canonical `Documentation` component: it is Red Hat docs\n42\tcontent-process work spanning the whole GA graduation rather than a single\n43\tcomponent's code surface. All other epics map one-to-one to the strategy's\n44\taffected components; no non-obvious mappings were needed.\n45\t\n46\t## Dependency DAG\n47\t\n48\t```mermaid\n49\tgraph TD\n50\t    E001[\"E001 GA-readiness assessment (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 API stability + GA hardening\"]\n51\t    E002 --> E004[\"E004 TrustyAI guardrails supported integration\"]\n52\t    E002 --> E006[\"E006 GA documentation uplift\"]\n53\t    E003[\"E003 production support posture\"] --> E006\n54\t    E004 --> E006\n55\t    E005[\"E005 Dashboard default-on\"] --> E006\n56\t```\n57\t\n58\t## DAG Justification\n59\t\n60\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n61\t|------|------|-----------|\n62\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream scope) | The strategy's open question states the GA scope depends on the assessment — until it lands, the size of the stability and hardening work is an estimate. True gate with action `rewrite`: the assessment's findings may add remediation scope (error handling, scale limits) beyond the stated API-stability work. The outcome set is unbounded (a full gap inventory), so no conditional branches — phased decomposition via the gate. |\n63\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer serial) | The TrustyAI guardrails integration consumes the Llama Stack safety / provider-plugin API surface; it must be validated against the versioned, stable surface this epic publishes, not the TP-era one. |\n64\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation describes what was built. |\n65\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation describes what was built. |\n66\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation describes what was built. |\n67\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation describes what was built. |\n68\t\n69\tDeliberate non-edges:\n70\t\n71\t- **E001 ↛ E003**: HLR-3's deliverables (SLO targets, backport policy,\n72\t  supported configuration matrix) are fully specified by the strategy; the\n73\t  assessment's inventory (API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error\n74\t  handling) refines the hardening epic, not the support-posture definition.\n75\t  They run in parallel.\n76\t- **E002 ↛ E005**: the Dashboard change is a default/flag flip in a different\n77\t  repo (odh-dashboard); the Dashboard reads the existing\n78\t  LlamaStackDistribution CR and builds against no artifact this strategy\n79\t  produces. Rule 14 parallel. Release-timing coordination (default-on should\n80\t  land with the GA release, not before) is recorded as a risk on the\n81\t  Dashboard epic, not as a DAG edge.\n82\t- **No onboarding epics**: all three components are already in the platform\n83\t  inventory and Konflux-onboarded per architecture context, so no\n84\t  repo/license/konflux onboarding chain is generated (Rule 20 does not apply).\n85\t\n86\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n87\t\n88\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic |\n89\t|-----|----------|-------------|------|\n90\t| HLR-1 | P0 | A GA-readiness assessment inventories the gaps between current TP quality and GA bar (API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error handling), producing the definitive GA scope. | RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 |\n91\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Published API surface carries a stability guarantee: versioned APIs, deprecation policy, and a tested upgrade path from the last two TP releases. | RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 |\n92\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Production support posture is in place: documented SLO targets, backport policy, and supported configuration matrix. | RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 |\n93\t| HLR-4 | P1 | Safety guardrails via TrustyAI are a supported, documented integration (not an experimental flag) for GA distributions. | RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 |\n94\t| HLR-5 | P1 | The Dashboard enables Llama Stack management by default for entitled clusters (no TP opt-in gate). | RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 |\n95\t| HLR-6 | P2 | Documentation is uplifted to GA standards, including migration notes from TP. | RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 |\n96\t\n97\tFull coverage: every HLR maps to exactly one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n98\tThe gap-remediation work implied by the problem statement (\"closing the\n99\tquality gaps that TP status currently excuses\") is executed by\n100\tRHAISTRAT-9014-E002 together with HLR-2 — same component, same team, one\n101\tlogical change (GA hardening of the published surface), and its concrete\n102\tscope cannot be known before the assessment resolves, so it cannot be\n103\tscheduled as a standalone epic.\n104\t\n105\t## Health Warnings\n106\t\n107\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n108\t  Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria,\n109\t  and no NFRs. Owner teams were assigned from the architecture-context owning\n110\t  teams (Llama Stack → RHOAI GenAI, TrustyAI → TrustyAI, Dashboard → RHOAI\n111\t  Dashboard); the documentation epic is assumed owned by RHOAI GenAI as owner\n112\t  of the primary subject component. Epic acceptance criteria were derived\n113\t  from HLR text.\n114\t- **Planning note (epic size)**: the API stability and GA hardening epic\n115\t  (RHAISTRAT-9014-E002) is above the ~2-week epic-size guideline by the\n116\t  strategy's own admission — the open question states the size of the\n117\t  stability and hardening work is an estimate until the assessment lands.\n118\t  Kept as one epic: the hardening is a single logical change over one\n119\t  published surface by one team, and its concrete scope cannot be enumerated\n120\t  before the assessment resolves, so a pre-emptive split would be arbitrary.\n121\t  **Re-split trigger**: re-split this epic if the assessment's definitive GA\n122\t  scope enumerates remediation items exceeding one sprint of effort or\n123\t  spanning distinct surfaces (e.g. scale-limit test infrastructure vs.\n124\t  API-surface changes) — split along those surface boundaries.\n125\t- **Release coordination (not a DAG edge)**: the Dashboard default-on change\n126\t  should ship with the GA graduation, not expose TP quality to all entitled\n127\t  users early. Recorded as a risk on the Dashboard epic; there is no code\n128\t  artifact dependency.\n129\t- **Priority note**: the documentation epic carries P0 (strategy priority\n130\t  Critical → P0 per the docs-authoring priority exception) even though its\n131\t  HLR is P2 — this is the derivation rule at work, not an inversion.\n132\t\n133\t## Tiered Delivery\n134\t\n135\tNot applicable. The strategy declares no external dependency with uncertain\n136\ttiming (no dependencies table; all components are in-platform and\n137\tKonflux-onboarded per architecture context), so there is no Tier 1 / Tier 2\n138\tsplit.\n139\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a98f47588918b14e","agent_id":"a7c43dc9dd403e7b9","session_id":"0204fee7-ce49-4572-ac85-f81f526b0c75","uuid":"f0ef4883-0463-4f47-9e2c-9be839c2c568","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:17:56.003Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-be4c6e7f3a5f88bd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
