{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9cf170e03e49485f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014: Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n11\t\n12\t## Triage\n13\t\n14\tFull decomposition. The strategy is not below-threshold: it spans three\n15\tcomponents (Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard) owned by three distinct teams,\n16\tits HLRs span P0/P1/P2, and it contains a genuine unknown — HLR-1's\n17\tGA-readiness assessment produces the definitive GA scope, which changes what\n18\tthe downstream stability and hardening epic must do. It is not\n19\tdocumentation-only: all three affected components need code or configuration\n20\tchanges.\n21\t\n22\t## Epic List\n23\t\n24\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n25\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | Llama Stack GA-readiness assessment | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantee and GA hardening | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | TrustyAI guardrails as a supported Llama Stack integration | Implementation | TrustyAI | P1 |\n30\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Dashboard Llama Stack management default-on for entitled clusters | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n31\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | P0 |\n32\t\n33\tPriority note for RHAISTRAT-9014-E006: per the docs-authoring priority\n34\texception its priority is derived from the strategy frontmatter\n35\t(`priority: Critical` → P0). It does not push priority upstream to the\n36\timplementations it depends on.\n37\t\n38\t## Component Mapping\n39\t\n40\tAll epic `component` values are verbatim canonical names from\n41\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`. RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 (documentation uplift)\n42\tuses the canonical `Documentation` component: it is Red Hat docs\n43\tcontent-process work spanning the whole GA graduation rather than a single\n44\tcomponent's code surface. All other epics map one-to-one to the strategy's\n45\taffected components; no non-obvious mappings were needed.\n46\t\n47\t## Dependency DAG\n48\t\n49\t```mermaid\n50\tgraph TD\n51\t    E001[\"E001 GA-readiness assessment (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"E002 API stability + GA hardening\"]\n52\t    E002 --> E004[\"E004 TrustyAI guardrails supported integration\"]\n53\t    E002 --> E006[\"E006 GA documentation uplift\"]\n54\t    E003[\"E003 production support posture\"] --> E006\n55\t    E004 --> E006\n56\t    E005[\"E005 Dashboard default-on\"] --> E006\n57\t```\n58\t\n59\t## DAG Justification\n60\t\n61\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n62\t|------|------|-----------|\n63\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream scope) | The strategy's open question states the GA scope depends on the assessment — until it lands, the size of the stability and hardening work is an estimate. True gate with action `rewrite`: the assessment's findings may add remediation scope (error handling, scale limits) beyond the stated API-stability work. The outcome set is unbounded (a full gap inventory), so no conditional branches — phased decomposition via the gate. |\n64\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer serial) | The TrustyAI guardrails integration consumes the Llama Stack safety / provider-plugin API surface; it must be validated against the versioned, stable surface this epic publishes, not the TP-era one. |\n65\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation describes what was built. |\n66\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation describes what was built. |\n67\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation describes what was built. |\n68\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring last) | Documentation describes what was built. |\n69\t\n70\tDeliberate non-edges:\n71\t\n72\t- **E001 ↛ E003**: HLR-3's deliverables (SLO targets, backport policy,\n73\t  supported configuration matrix) are fully specified by the strategy; the\n74\t  assessment's inventory (API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error\n75\t  handling) refines the hardening epic, not the support-posture definition.\n76\t  They run in parallel.\n77\t- **E002 ↛ E005**: the Dashboard change is a default/flag flip in a different\n78\t  repo (odh-dashboard); the Dashboard reads the existing\n79\t  LlamaStackDistribution CR and builds against no artifact this strategy\n80\t  produces. Rule 14 parallel. Release-timing coordination (default-on should\n81\t  land with the GA release, not before) is recorded as a risk on the\n82\t  Dashboard epic, not as a DAG edge.\n83\t- **No onboarding epics**: all three components are already in the platform\n84\t  inventory and Konflux-onboarded per architecture context, so no\n85\t  repo/license/konflux onboarding chain is generated (Rule 20 does not apply).\n86\t\n87\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n88\t\n89\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic |\n90\t|-----|----------|-------------|------|\n91\t| HLR-1 | P0 | A GA-readiness assessment inventories the gaps between current TP quality and GA bar (API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error handling), producing the definitive GA scope. | RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 |\n92\t| HLR-2 | P0 | Published API surface carries a stability guarantee: versioned APIs, deprecation policy, and a tested upgrade path from the last two TP releases. | RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 |\n93\t| HLR-3 | P0 | Production support posture is in place: documented SLO targets, backport policy, and supported configuration matrix. | RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 |\n94\t| HLR-4 | P1 | Safety guardrails via TrustyAI are a supported, documented integration (not an experimental flag) for GA distributions. | RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 |\n95\t| HLR-5 | P1 | The Dashboard enables Llama Stack management by default for entitled clusters (no TP opt-in gate). | RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 |\n96\t| HLR-6 | P2 | Documentation is uplifted to GA standards, including migration notes from TP. | RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 |\n97\t\n98\tFull coverage: every HLR maps to exactly one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n99\tThe gap-remediation work implied by the problem statement (\"closing the\n100\tquality gaps that TP status currently excuses\") is executed by\n101\tRHAISTRAT-9014-E002 together with HLR-2 — same component, same team, one\n102\tlogical change (GA hardening of the published surface), and its concrete\n103\tscope cannot be known before the assessment resolves, so it cannot be\n104\tscheduled as a standalone epic.\n105\t\n106\tRule 19 note (fail-mode/security Investigation): HLR-4 sits in the guardrails\n107\tsafety domain, but no fail-mode/security Investigation epic is generated.\n108\tRationale: this strategy promotes an existing, already-integrated experimental\n109\tTrustyAI guardrails capability to a supported configuration — it is hardening\n110\tof a known integration, not the design of a new safety-critical capability\n111\twhose failure modes are undiscovered. The fail-mode concern is absorbed by\n112\tRHAISTRAT-9014-E004's hardening scope (error handling, configuration\n113\tvalidation, CI integration tests) and pinned by its acceptance criterion that\n114\tguardrails failure behavior in a supported configuration is defined and\n115\ttested.\n116\t\n117\t## Health Warnings\n118\t\n119\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): the strategy has no Impacted\n120\t  Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria,\n121\t  and no NFRs. Owner teams were assigned from the architecture-context owning\n122\t  teams (Llama Stack → RHOAI GenAI, TrustyAI → TrustyAI, Dashboard → RHOAI\n123\t  Dashboard); the documentation epic is assumed owned by RHOAI GenAI as owner\n124\t  of the primary subject component. Epic acceptance criteria were derived\n125\t  from HLR text.\n126\t- **Planning note (epic size)**: the API stability and GA hardening epic\n127\t  (RHAISTRAT-9014-E002) is above the ~2-week epic-size guideline by the\n128\t  strategy's own admission — the open question states the size of the\n129\t  stability and hardening work is an estimate until the assessment lands.\n130\t  Kept as one epic: the hardening is a single logical change over one\n131\t  published surface by one team, and its concrete scope cannot be enumerated\n132\t  before the assessment resolves, so a pre-emptive split would be arbitrary.\n133\t  **Re-split trigger**: re-split this epic if the assessment's definitive GA\n134\t  scope enumerates remediation items exceeding one sprint of effort or\n135\t  spanning distinct surfaces (e.g. scale-limit test infrastructure vs.\n136\t  API-surface changes) — split along those surface boundaries.\n137\t- **Release coordination (not a DAG edge)**: the Dashboard default-on change\n138\t  should ship with the GA graduation, not expose TP quality to all entitled\n139\t  users early. Recorded as a risk on the Dashboard epic; there is no code\n140\t  artifact dependency.\n141\t- **Priority note**: the documentation epic carries P0 (strategy priority\n142\t  Critical → P0 per the docs-authoring priority exception) even though its\n143\t  HLR is P2 — this is the derivation rule at work, not an inversion.\n144\t\n145\t## Tiered Delivery\n146\t\n147\tNot applicable. The strategy declares no external dependency with uncertain\n148\ttiming (no dependencies table; all components are in-platform and\n149\tKonflux-onboarded per architecture context), so there is no Tier 1 / Tier 2\n150\tsplit.\n151\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9de9ef08aa817306","agent_id":"a3f3f864f5c7bc769","session_id":"0204fee7-ce49-4572-ac85-f81f526b0c75","uuid":"88a3